International Conference on Application Specific Array Processors

The 1994 International Conference on Application Specific Array
Processors (ASAP-94) will be held at the Fairmont Hotel in San
Francisco, California, USA on August 22 through 24, 1994.  This is
the eighth in a series of conferences, which started with the
International Workshop on Systolic Arrays in Oxford, England in
1986, continued with the International Conferences on Systolic
Arrays in San Diego, USA in 1988 and Killarney, Ireland in 1989, and
evolved to the International Conferences on Application Specific
Array Processors in Princeton, New Jersey in 1990, Barcelona, Spain
in 1991, Berkeley, California in 1992, and Venice, Italy in 1993.
Through the years, the scope of the research presented at the
conferences has evolved to encompass new trends and results in
various areas of application specific computing.  This conference is
the leading forum for researchers from universities and industry who
are interested in the fundamental aspects of application specific
computing systems.

The program includes a Keynote speech, Cellular Neural Network
Universal Machine: An Analogic Supercomputer on a Chip by Professor
Leon O. Chua, from the University of California, Berkeley.

>From Berkely Research Highlights: Bionic eyes could become reality, thanks to a  
radically new kind of computer.

"While our retina takes a fifth of a second to process all the information from  
one image, the CNN chip can do it in a few millionths of a second.  ...  The  
development of CNN is about equivalent to the invention of the microprocessor,  
which set off the PC industry," says Professor Chua. "I think this too will  
spawn a new industry."

