Russell, your message didn't seem sarcastic to me at all; it seemed quite
sincere. Alan G. Isaac's foregoing message makes plain that he is here to
clear up some confusion about, start a discussion of, or spoil for a fight
over, _basics_ of libertarian politics.  It appears that he is arguing for
goverment intervention - "a serious enforcement problem arises" - in the
privately decided matter of one's children's education. He suggests that
the state should enforce the proper education of  children. Surely,  as an
associate professor of  economics, he knows that the state taxes people to
pay for enforcement of its laws.

I claim that I have no financial responsibility for other people's
children, and I refuse to be compelled to pay for other people's failures
to fulfill their duty to their children. This claim has nothing to do with
whether I believe children are property of their parents. ( Choosing to
take care of someone does not mean that you own her!) That's a red herring
if ever I sniffed one.

Don't take the bait, friends.


