From: hibbert@netcom.com
Date: Thu, 30 Jun 1994 12:40:58 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-207] Nanotechnology and uncertainty 

Gregory,

The weakness in your argument is that you assume that someone will get some
discrete jump in ability (and you don't care whether it's to 100 brainpower or
10000 brainpower) some time earlier than everyone else (and you don't care whether
it's a year earlier or 3 months earlier).  What actually happens in the real world
it that everyone moves forward by steps, and so in the year in which someone
invents a 1000 brainpower machine, some of the other competitors will have
invented 900 brainpower machines 3 months earlier, and others will come out with
1500 brainpower machines 3 months later.  No one gets to be a factor of 1000 ahead
of everyone else at any point.  Someone is always the first to reach any
particular milestone, but everyone else was close a little while ago, or will do
better in a little bit.

Chriss

