From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Thu, 30 Jun 1994 17:11:11 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-208] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears? 

Gregory Sullivan writes:
>I agree that integration of uploaded beings would reduce the likelyhood of
>war. Based on my comments above it will be hard.  Here is a crude analogy :
>uploads and humans in the same society will be like humans and monkeys in
>the same society. They are not equal participants. In fact the
>``participation'' of monkeys in a human society with respect to power would
>be illusory. I guess you reject this reasoning. I look forward to the next
>Extropy.

I do not think integration is an either/or matter, and do not claim
uploads will be just like ordinary people.  I agree that they will be
radical in many ways.  Rather, I think integration is a matter of
degree, and the more integrated the better.  If all monkeys lived on
an island somewhere, I think their long-term survival would be much
more at risk than when many of them live in human zoos, and as pets in
human homes.  Monkeys are more integrated than they might be, and that
helps them.

>3) Maybe the delay between breakthroughs of different factions is smaller
>then that given in the scenario. The development testing and ramp up of
>technology allows competing factions to keep closer tabs on on another.
>Maybe some how everything speeds up and there is no less uncertainty than in
>the past relative to the new time scale.  ...
>
>Even if humans are not in control consider this.  Many interlocking
>technologies will all be changing along with weapons technology. ...
>
>I am unconvinced that these technologies will somehow move together so that
>a stable balance is maintained and warring factions will face uncertainty
>comparable to historical levels of uncertainty.

[I'll here echo Chris Hibbert's comments.]  War has gone on for at
least ten thousand years, and technology has changed radically many
times over that period, without any special synchronization of the
development of different kinds of technology.  Do you expect we should
all have been dead by now?  If not, what is different about nanotech?

Robin Hanson

